WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.130
- [Instructor] We're
told that we wanna factor

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:03.800
the following expression and they ask us

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.190
which pattern can we use
to factor the expression?

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.060
And U and V are either constant integers

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.250
or single-variable expressions.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.610
So we'll do this one together and then

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:13.730
we'll have a few more examples where

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.830
I'll encourage you to pause the video.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:17.760
So when they're talking about patterns

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.287
they're really saying, "Hey, can we say

00:00:20.287 --> 00:00:22.727
"that some of these can generally form

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:25.027
"a pattern that matches what we have here

00:00:25.027 --> 00:00:27.137
"and then we can use
that pattern to factor

00:00:27.137 --> 00:00:28.080
"it into one of these forms?"

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.410
What do I mean by that?

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.990
Well, let's just imagine something like

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.760
U plus V squared.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.380
We've squared binomials in the past.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.790
This is going to be equal to U squared

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.980
plus two times the product of these terms

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.213
so two U V and then plus V squared.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.230
Now, when you look at
this polynomial right

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.830
over here, it actually has this form

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:53.300
if you look at it carefully.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.950
How can it have this form?

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.730
Well, if we view U squared as nine X

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.480
to the eighth then that means that U is,

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.810
and let me write it as a capital U,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.110
U is equal to three X to the fourth.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.830
'Cause notice if you square this

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.240
you're gonna get nine X to the eighth.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.960
So this right over here is U squared.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.940
And if we said that V squared is equal

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.810
to Y squared, so if this
is capital V squared

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.450
then that means that V is equal to Y.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.200
And then this would have
to be two times U V.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.240
Is it?

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.100
Well, see if I multiply
U times V I get three

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.460
X to the fourth Y and then two times that

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.490
is indeed six X to the fourth Y.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.460
So this right over here is two U V.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.210
So notice this polynomial,
this higher-degree

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.750
polynomial can be
expressed in this pattern,

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:51.490
which means it can be factored this way.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.410
So when they say which pattern can we use

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.820
to factor this expression,
well, I would use

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.430
a pattern for U plus V squared.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.320
So I would go with that
choice right over there.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.923
Let's do a few more examples.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.850
So here once again we're
told the same thing.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.440
We're given a different expression

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.850
and they're asking us
what pattern can we use

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.780
to factor the expression?

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.240
So I have these three terms here.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.320
It looks like maybe I could use, I can see

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.153
a perfect square here.

00:02:21.153 --> 00:02:22.410
Let's see if that works.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:27.410
If this is U squared, if this is U squared

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.730
then that means that
U is going to be equal

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.940
to two X to the third power.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.940
And if this is V squared then that means

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.750
that V is equal to five.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.510
Now is this equal to two times U V?

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.340
Well, let's see, two times
U V would be equal to,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.490
well, you're not gonna have any Y in it

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.360
so this is not going to be two U V.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.360
So this actually is not fitting
the perfect square pattern.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.760
So we could rule this out.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.210
And both of these are
perfect squares of some form.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.010
One just has a, I guess
you'd say adding V.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.970
The other one is subtracting V.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.920
This right over here,
if I were to multiply

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.430
this out, this is going to be equal to,

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.520
this is a difference of squares
and we've seen this before.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.840
This is U squared minus V squared.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.480
So you wouldn't have a
three-term polynomial like that.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:15.970
So we could rule that one out.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:19.200
So I would pick that we can't
use any of the patterns.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.430
Let's do yet another example.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.140
And I encourage you,
pause the video and see

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.190
if you can work this one out on your own.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.460
So the same idea, they wanna factor

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.230
the following expression.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.500
And this one essentially has two terms.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.763
We have a term here and
we have a term here.

00:03:36.938 --> 00:03:38.780
They both look like they are the square

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.820
of something and we have
a difference of squares.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.570
So this is making me feel pretty good

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.690
about this pattern but
let's see if that works out.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.380
Remember, U plus V
times U minus V is equal

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.430
to U squared minus V squared.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:56.300
So if this is equal to U
squared then that means

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.670
that capital U is equal to six X squared.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:02.433
That works.

00:04:02.433 --> 00:04:05.570
And if this is equal to V squared, well,

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.000
that means that V is
equal to Y plus three.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.870
So this is fitting this
pattern right over here.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.500
And they're just asking
us to say what pattern

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.350
can we use to factor the expression.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.460
They're not asking us
to actually factor it.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:18.030
So we'll just pick this choice.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.690
But once you identify
the pattern it's actually

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.300
pretty straightforward to factor it

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.210
because if you say this
is just going to factor

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.670
into U plus V times U
minus V, well, U plus V

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:32.490
is going to be six X squared plus V plus Y

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.720
plus three times U minus V.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.150
U is six X squared.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.570
Minus V is minus, we could write minus Y

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.170
plus three or we could
distribute the negative sign.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.710
But either way this might
make it a little bit

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.290
clearer what we just did.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:50.530
We used a pattern to
factor this higher-degree

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:52.260
polynomial which is essentially just

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.090
a difference of squares.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.793
Let's do one last example.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.930
So here once again we
wanna factor an expression.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.200
Which pattern can we use?

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.093
Pause the video.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.450
All right, so we have two terms here.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:08.850
So it looks like it might be a difference

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:13.020
of squares if we set U is equal to seven

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.250
and then this would be U U squared.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.720
But then what can we square to get 10 X

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.110
to the third power?

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:24.060
Remember, we wanna have
integer exponents here.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.890
And the square root of
10 X to the third power,

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.070
if I were to take the square root of 10 X

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.280
to the third power it would be something

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.890
a little bit involved
like the square root of 10

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:38.160
times X times the square root of X

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.720
to the third power and
I'm not going to get

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.230
an integer exponent here.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.350
So it doesn't look like I can
express this as V squared.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:48.330
So I would go with that we
can't use any of the patterns.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.163
And we're done.

